Ours (dsv41 recipe), Apache-2.0, against vLLM main 6e517b15 (touches no file of patches 0001-0013).
mHC's coefficient overlap on GB10 (VLLM_DSV41_MHC_OVERLAP=1; default off). In FULL decode graphs of up to 16 tokens,
vLLM's V4.1 decoder layer can compute each sublayer's mix statistics (the 24 x 20480 prenorm dots + 20 Sinkhorn
iterations) on a side stream and only the normalized input on the main stream, because the reference collapses a
sublayer's input with the *previous* sublayer's pre-mix: the new mixes are needed only at this sublayer's post
(mhc_pre_delayed_overlap / mhc_shifted_post_pre with a stream). supports_mhc_overlap admits family 100 only; on GB10
the path's kernels exist too (DeepGEMM's tf32_hc_prenorm_gemm has an sm120 build, already in our profiles, and the
split pre-norm epilogue is the TileLang kernel the default path runs), while the SM100-only pieces (mega mHC, the fused
TP=4 all-reduce + mHC) keep their own checks. TensorFold moved the same work to a side stream on these boxes
(github.com/bertholomus/TensorFold, branch deepseek-v41-tp2, a8e1a5c / bd0024d: drafted forward 46.1 -> 45.5 ms).
The mix statistics go through DeepGEMM's TF32 GEMM (as on B200) instead of the TileLang fused path: not bit-identical,
check KLD and the oracle before turning it on.

